Connect the drawing to a defined dimensional measurement (@mech-dimensional-entry: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @mechanical-engineering, #source "../sources/Engineering-Drawings-for-Measurement-NPL.lingua#r_48E0H38P8GEB0XPX68MYXGGA48", #mechanical-engineering "../../../inquiry/metrology/applications/Dimensions-geometry-and-surfaces.lingua#r_66KYHH97TER9KH7NJJASWS9K5K", #hephaestus-core "../../core/Connect-an-assembly-prediction-to-a-bounded-inspection-decision.lingua#r_0AE23276AAB728BEDBFC0AC47E") { r_101DKG7DGDQ1Q0GMRBRHBFHC4Q
**Read:** [Engineering drawings for measurement — NPL Guide 79](../sources/Engineering-Drawings-for-Measurement-NPL.lingua#r_48E0H38P8GEB0XPX68MYXGGA48), inspection, then the shared dimensional-metrology Record linked next, including its complete guide and uncertainty examples.

Translate each critical drawing requirement into a measurand, reference, sampling scheme and decision. Name the surface or fitted feature, reference temperature, alignment, contact force and data-processing convention. A display value alone does not establish these.

Choose a technique whose uncertainty and spatial coverage suit the decision. A caliper, indicator, gauge, coordinate machine and optical system observe different parts of the geometry. Access, fixturing and part deformation belong in the method.

**Work:** Write an inspection method for the chosen support or carriage. Predict at least three important uncertainty contributions before collecting readings. Explain how an unmeasured form deviation could affect the assembly even if the sampled dimensions agree. Continue through the shared metrology study before interpreting an acceptance result.

**Return:** [Mechanical engineering and machine design](../Mechanical-engineering-and-machine-design.lingua#r_0017YZ63HZBZ4P4WD8DECVWE4R).
**Continue:** [Dimensions, form, surfaces, and nanometre-scale comparisons](../../../inquiry/metrology/applications/Dimensions-geometry-and-surfaces.lingua#r_66KYHH97TER9KH7NJJASWS9K5K).


**Second engineering-block reading:** First identify the intended gap quantity, physical references, conditions, access and missing form information, then use the next common lesson’s explicitly stipulated error-bound model. NPL Guide 79 printed pages 70–74 support the inspection/uncertainty questions; the historical standard references do not establish a current normative decision rule. The original complete dimensional-metrology continuation and broader uncertainty work remain available with their preparation.

**Continue in the second engineering block:** [Connect an assembly prediction to a bounded inspection decision](../../core/Connect-an-assembly-prediction-to-a-bounded-inspection-decision.lingua#r_0AE23276AAB728BEDBFC0AC47E).

